</nav><main id="main-content"><header class="hero"><p class="eyebrow">Product design · Telegram learning prototype</p><h1 class="hero-title">Two Sticks</h1><p class="hero__line">Write the character, without leaving the chat.</p><p class="hero__intro">Two Sticks was a Telegram prototype for learning Chinese characters. A 2024 MPGU diploma supplied the pedagogical ground. I turned that ground into a product and built the bot, the API, and the handwriting web app inside Telegram. The implementation originally used the name Chinese Bee.</p><dl class="facts"><div><dt>Role</dt><dd>Product direction, UX/UI, bot, API, handwriting app</dd></div><div><dt>When</dt><dd>2024, with a diploma project at MPGU</dd></div><div><dt>Research</dt><dd>2024 MPGU diploma: copybooks, methods, competitors, masterclass</dd></div><div><dt>Outcome</dt><dd>Shipped prototype, no longer hosted</dd></div></dl><figure class="hero-stage"><div class="hero-stage__phones"><Image src={startScreen} alt="Two Sticks introduction and first learning prompt in Telegram." widths={[240, 360, 480]} sizes="(max-width: 760px) 29vw, 240px" format="webp" quality={85} /><Image src={searchScreen} alt="Two Sticks word search result with character details and a save action." widths={[240, 360, 480]} sizes="(max-width: 760px) 29vw, 240px" format="webp" quality={85} /><Image src={writeScreen} alt="Two Sticks handwriting Web App with a drawn character and recognition score." widths={[240, 360, 480]} sizes="(max-width: 760px) 29vw, 240px" format="webp" quality={85} /></div><Image src={beeIcon} alt="" widths={[96, 144, 180]} sizes="(max-width: 760px) 4.5rem, 7.5rem" format="webp" class="hero-stage__icon" /><figcaption>Working screens from the Telegram learning loop.</figcaption></figure></header><section class="chapter" id="context" data-index-section>
  <header class="chapter__label">
    <span>01</span>
    <p>Context</p>
  </header>
  <div class="chapter__body">
    <h2>Recognition was easy to practise. Writing was still left on paper.</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Learning a Chinese character means connecting shape, stroke order, sound, structure, and meaning. A dictionary can explain the character. It cannot make the learner reproduce it.
      </p>
      <p>
        Printed copybooks keep the hand involved, but they are fixed, easy to lose, and slow to check. The product opportunity was not another language course. It was a short path from finding a character to writing it inside the messenger the learner already opened.
      </p>
    </div>
    <dl class="scope">
      <div><dt>Goal</dt><dd>Let a beginner look up a character, save it, understand it, recall it, and write it without installing another learning app.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>I owned</dt><dd>Product direction, information architecture, interface, Telegram bot, FastAPI, PostgreSQL word set, Vue handwriting app, and recognition scoring.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Research authors</dt><dd>Смакова, Шаталова, and Шинкарева. Their 2024 MPGU diploma was called Two Sticks / Две палочки.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Constraint</dt><dd>Stay inside Telegram and keep writing in the core loop. A friendly dictionary was not enough.</dd></div>
    </dl>
    <blockquote>How can a saved character travel all the way to the learner’s hand?</blockquote>
  </div>
</section><section class="chapter" id="research" data-index-section>
  <header class="chapter__label">
    <span>02</span>
    <p>Ground</p>
  </header>
  <div class="chapter__body">
    <h2>The pedagogical research was not mine. Translating it into behaviour was.</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        I worked from the diploma team’s study of copybook problems, memorisation methods, Skritter, Duolingo, HelloChinese, Telegram bots, and an open-day masterclass. Fifteen of twenty participants expressed interest. I do not present that work as my own usability study.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="qa">
      <p class="research-label">Research ground → product behaviour</p>
      <dl>
        <div><dt>Copywork</dt><dd>Writing had to remain an action. The loop ends on a canvas, not on a correct multiple-choice answer.</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Cards</dt><dd>A personal saved set feeds flashcards in the same chat instead of creating a separate course.</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Radicals and structure</dt><dd>The notebook sheet exposes how a character is composed before the learner draws it.</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Mnemonics and examples</dt><dd>The generated sheet keeps shape, meaning, similar characters, and use together.</dd></div>
      </dl>
    </div>

    <div class="compare">
      <p class="research-label">The useful competitive gap</p>
      <article>
        <h3>Skritter</h3>
        <p>The writing specialist and the quality bar for stroke practice. It goes deeper than Two Sticks and requires a separate paid product.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <h3>Telegram bots</h3>
        <p>Easy to enter, but mostly lists, vocabulary, and commands. The missing piece was copywork connected to a personal set.</p>
      </article>
    </div>

    <div class="finding">
      <p>The product question</p>
      <strong>Could Telegram remain the shell while writing, not translation, became the centre?</strong>
    </div>
  </div>
</section><section class="chapter chapter--dark" id="direction" data-index-section>
  <header class="chapter__label">
    <span>03</span>
    <p>Direction</p>
  </header>
  <div class="chapter__body">
    <h2>I cut a broad diploma brief into one loop that could ship.</h2>
    <ol class="principles">
      <li><span>1</span><div><h3>Telegram is the shell</h3><p>Search, saved words, flashcards, prompts, and notebook delivery stay in the chat a learner already uses.</p></div></li>
      <li><span>2</span><div><h3>The saved set is the spine</h3><p>One personal list feeds recall, notebook work, and dictation. Practice cannot start from an empty feature.</p></div></li>
      <li><span>3</span><div><h3>Writing closes the loop</h3><p>Search and cards prepare the character. The Web App asks the hand to reproduce it and returns a narrow recognition score.</p></div></li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="chapter chapter--wide" id="journey" data-index-section>
  <header class="chapter__label">
    <span>04</span>
    <p>Product</p>
  </header>
  <div class="chapter__body">
    <h2>One saved character moves through four kinds of work.</h2>
    <p class="wide-copy">
      The product is not a list of bot commands. It is a loop: find, keep, understand, recall, write. These are the working surfaces I built around that loop.
    </p>
  </div>

  <ol class="journey-map">
    <li>
      <p>01 Find + keep</p>
      <h3>Start from a word the learner already knows</h3>
      <dl>
        <div><dt>Decision</dt><dd>Search returns matches, then a details card with Chinese, pinyin, meaning, HSK level, and Save.</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Why</dt><dd>A lookup that cannot feed practice is a dead end. Saving stays on the character card.</dd></div>
      </dl>
      <figure><Image src={searchScreen} alt="Two Sticks word search result with character details and a save action." widths={[240, 360, 480]} sizes="(max-width: 760px) 46vw, 168px" format="webp" quality={85} /></figure>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>02 Recall</p>
      <h3>Make the set large enough to produce a real choice</h3>
      <dl>
        <div><dt>Decision</dt><dd>Flashcards use the learner’s saved words and wait until five exist. The empty state says exactly how many are missing.</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Why</dt><dd>A flashcard feature with no plausible alternatives is interface theatre.</dd></div>
      </dl>
      <figure><Image src={flashcardScreen} alt="Two Sticks flashcard practice using the learner’s saved characters." widths={[240, 360, 480]} sizes="(max-width: 760px) 46vw, 168px" format="webp" quality={85} /></figure>
    </li>
    <li class="is-sheet">
      <p>03 Understand</p>
      <h3>Parse the character before asking for memory</h3>
      <dl>
        <div><dt>Decision</dt><dd>Generate a notebook sheet with structure, elements, similar characters, mnemonic, examples, and a writing grid.</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Why</dt><dd>The sheet turns pedagogical terms into a working object the learner can keep in the same chat.</dd></div>
      </dl>
      <figure><Image src={notebookFilled} alt="Filled notebook sheet for 教 with structure, stroke order, similar characters, mnemonic, and writing grid." widths={[480, 720, 960]} sizes="(max-width: 760px) 92vw, 320px" format="webp" quality={85} /></figure>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>04 Write</p>
      <h3>Ask the hand, then score the result honestly</h3>
      <dl>
        <div><dt>Decision</dt><dd>The Telegram Web App draws from the same saved set: prompt, canvas, clear, check, next.</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Limit</dt><dd>The API recognises similarity to the target character. It does not teach or verify stroke order.</dd></div>
      </dl>
      <figure><Image src={writeScreen} alt="Handwriting Web App with a drawn character, Clear, Check, Next word, and an accuracy score." widths={[240, 360, 480]} sizes="(max-width: 760px) 46vw, 168px" format="webp" quality={85} /></figure>
    </li>
  </ol>

  <div class="validation-board">
    <article class="validation-case">
      <p><strong>Historical status.</strong> The prototype is no longer hosted. Keeping the bot, API, and Web App online requires paid infrastructure, so this case study documents the interaction through surviving screens and public source code.</p>
    </article>
  </div>
</section><section class="chapter chapter--wide chapter--outcome" id="outcome" data-index-section>
  <header class="chapter__label">
    <span>05</span>
    <p>Outcome</p>
  </header>
  <div class="chapter__body">
    <h2>A working bot, API, and handwriting app. Not the whole diploma brief.</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Two Sticks reached a working Russian Telegram prototype with a FastAPI backend and a Vue Web App. Search, details, save, a five-word flashcard gate, facts, a character primer, notebook sheets, and handwriting scoring were implemented and the source remains public.
      </p>
    </div>
    <dl class="scope">
      <div><dt>Shipped</dt><dd>Lookup, saved set, flashcards, notebook delivery, character guidance, and Web App dictation with an OCR score.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Did not ship</dt><dd>Live teacher supervision, an HSK statistics dashboard, and the planned growth engine. Those stayed in the wider diploma brief.</dd></div>
    </dl>
  </div>
  <div class="outcome-board">
    <figure><Image src={notebookBlank} alt="Blank Two Sticks notebook sheet ready for character study." widths={[640, 960, 1280, 1600]} sizes="(max-width: 760px) 94vw, 920px" format="webp" quality={85} /><figcaption><span>05.1</span> A generated copybook page that stays with the learner.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure><Image src={writeScreen} alt="Dictation Web App showing handwriting and an accuracy score." widths={[240, 360, 480, 640]} sizes="(max-width: 760px) 56vw, 360px" format="webp" quality={85} /><figcaption><span>05.2</span> The same saved set reaches the hand.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
</section><section class="chapter reflection" id="reflection" data-index-section>
  <header class="chapter__label">
    <span>06</span>
    <p>Reflection</p>
  </header>
  <div class="chapter__body">
    <h2>The useful part was not making a friendlier dictionary.</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>The product’s unit became a saved set a person could eventually write. Search, cards, and notebook sheets only matter because they feed that movement.</p>
      <p>I own the product direction, information architecture, interface, bot, API, and handwriting app. The diploma team owns the pedagogical research and the masterclass result. My contribution was translating their ground into behaviour and building it.</p>
      <p>The honest next measurement is whether people who save five characters begin dictation, and whether the generated notebook is used or skipped.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section></main><footer class="case-footer"><p>Two Sticks<br><em>Write the character, without leaving the chat.</em></p><nav aria-label="Two Sticks project links"><a href="https://github.com/KidPudel/chinesebee_bot">Bot source ↗</a><a href="https://github.com/KidPudel/chinesebee_api">API source ↗</a><a href="https://github.com/KidPudel/chinese-bee-dictation">Dictation source ↗</a><a href={sitePath('/work/')}>Back to work ↑</a><a href={sitePath('/case-studies/supergood/')}>SuperGood case study →</a></nav></footer>
</BaseDocument>

<script>
  document.documentElement.classList.add('has-scroll-stories');

  const activateFrame = (story: HTMLElement, index: number) => {
    story.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-story-frame]').forEach((frame) => {
      frame.classList.toggle('is-active', Number(frame.dataset.storyFrame) === index);
    });
    story.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-story-step]').forEach((step) => {
      step.classList.toggle('is-active', Number(step.dataset.storyStep) === index);
    });
  };

  document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-scroll-story]').forEach((story) => {
    if (story.dataset.storyReady) return;
    story.dataset.storyReady = 'true';

    const observer = new IntersectionObserver((entries) => {
      entries.forEach((entry) => {
        if (!entry.isIntersecting) return;
        const step = entry.target as HTMLElement;
        activateFrame(story, Number(step.dataset.storyStep));
      });
    }, { rootMargin: '-40% 0px -44%', threshold: 0 });

    story.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-story-step]').forEach((step) => observer.observe(step));
  });

  const indexLinks = Array.from(document.querySelectorAll<HTMLAnchorElement>('[data-index-link]'));
  const setCurrentSection = (id: string) => {
    indexLinks.forEach((link) => {
      if (link.dataset.indexLink === id) link.setAttribute('aria-current', 'location');
      else link.removeAttribute('aria-current');
    });
  };

  const sectionObserver = new IntersectionObserver((entries) => {
    entries.forEach((entry) => {
      if (entry.isIntersecting) setCurrentSection(entry.target.id);
    });
  }, { rootMargin: '-44% 0px -48%', threshold: 0 });

  document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-index-section]').forEach((section) => sectionObserver.observe(section));
</script>

